WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.300
We have a lot of choices
to make about our diet.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.180
Add to that, doing
the right thing

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.380
when it comes
to preventing

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.700
and treating a
chronic disease,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.040
fighting a virus,
or losing weight,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.440
and suddenly our
nutrition choices

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.840
can seem almost
overwhelming.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.240
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.460
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.560
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.540
Today, it all comes down

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.280
to the shape and
support of our body,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.920
the protection of our
organs and those things

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.360
without which we would quickly
become a gelatinous mass

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.220
— our bones!

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.540
In our first story,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.240
we look at how those
eating plant-based diets

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.619
may be so slim, that they suffer
from a loss of bone mass.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.840
Osteoporosis is estimated to affect
200 million people worldwide.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.180
Literally meaning "porous bone ,"
osteoporosis is a disease

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.240
characterized by reduced bone
formation, excessive bone loss,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.300
or a combination of both,
leading to bone fragility

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.920
and increased risk of fractures.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.940
And bone mineral density is the
most robust and consistent predictor

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.980
of osteoporotic fracture. What can
we eat to boost our bone density?

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.920
Well, we know that increased
consumption of plant foods

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.140
is associated with increased
bone mineral density.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.740
There's an extensive range of
micronutrients and phytochemicals

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.960
packaged within plants that can be
powerful promoters of bone health,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.360
so healthcare professionals
should be encouraged

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.820
to advise the increased
consumption of plant-based foods,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.260
particularly in mid-life,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.380
irrespective of the clients
underlying dietary pattern,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.020
meaning no matter how
much meat or junk they eat,

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.600
adding more healthy plant
foods may help prevent

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.760
the development of osteoporosis.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.360
On the other hand, a more
animal-source nutrient pattern

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.760
has been associated with
a higher risk of fractures,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.780
suggesting that a more animal-based
diet is related to bone fragility.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.340
So one would expect less osteoporosis
in those eating plant-based diets,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.660
but you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.980
"The Incidence of osteoporosis
in vegetarians and omnivores ,"

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.440
the first study published
nearly 50 years ago,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.800
and the density of the bones
that were measured was

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.680
significantly greater in the
vegetarians than the omnivores.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.460
In fact, the average bone densities
of the vegetarians in their '70s

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.060
was greater than the densities
of the meat eaters in their '50s.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.740
Bottom line, these results suggest
there's less likelihood of vegetarians

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.020
developing osteoporosis in old age.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.280
Turns out, though, that
the researchers screwed up.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.140
DEXA scanning, which
is what we use now,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.060
didn't come online until the 1980s.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.720
So the researchers were
just using regular x-rays

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.520
and they confused the readings,
such that darker bones on the x-ray

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.220
got a higher score, but that
actually means less bone,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.080
so their conclusion should
have been the opposite

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:49.920
of what they claimed.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.800
So vegetarians had
worse bone mineral density.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.180
Fast-forward about 40 years,
by which time nine studies

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.700
had been done on thousands
of individuals, and all in all,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.000
the results suggest that vegetarian
diets, particularly vegan diets,

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.240
are associated with lower
bone mineral density, but

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.380
the magnitude of the association
is clinically insignificant, meaning

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.920
the difference was so small as to not
really matter out in the real world,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.620
reinforcing the fact
that vegetarian diets

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.680
have no clinically detrimental
effect on bone health.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.280
And it is important to note
that the findings of lower

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.180
bone mineral density didn't fully
control for key confounding factors,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.100
such as for differences
in body weight.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.700
We know that people who are
obese have stronger bones. Why?

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.800
Because they're weight lifting 50 pounds
all day, every day, and maybe 100 pounds.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.380
If you walked around with a
100-pound backpack every day,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.180
your bones would grow stronger, too.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.820
That's how you build strong
bones: weight-bearing exercise.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.820
So people who weigh
more have denser bones.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.080
And vegetarians, and especially
vegans, have such low rates of obesity

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.260
that no wonder, on average they
would have lower bone density.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.660
The researchers didn't
take weight into account,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.740
but if the difference they found isn't
even clinically significant, who cares?

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.260
As of 2009, the answer to the question,

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.980
"Is vegetarianism a serious risk
factor for osteoporotic fracture?"

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.540
the answer was no. Vegetarianism
is not a serious risk factor.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:17.400
By 2018, the latest meta-analysis
on veganism, vegetarianism,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.460
and bone mineral density,
was up to 20 studies,

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.760
involving tens of thousands
of participants, and, again,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.520
lower bone mineral density
was found in studies

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.620
of vegetarians and vegans
compared to meat eaters.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.680
The researchers conclude that
vegetarian and vegan diets

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.800
need to be appropriately
planned to preserve their bones.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.860
But wait, did they account
for the obesity thing?

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.960
No, they did not.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.960
They just used what are
called crude risk ratios,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.780
meaning no adjustments for
confounding factors like weight,

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.080
so they didn't control for things
like age, smoking, obesity,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.920
exercise, and so their results
are really uninterpretable.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.220
But no one had gone through
the trouble of going back through

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.540
all those studies and making
the proper adjustments until now.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.220
The title gives it away: "Differences
in Bone Mineral Density between

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.700
Vegetarians and Nonvegetarians
Become Marginal when

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.340
Accounting for Differences
in Body Size Factors."

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.700
Yes, bone mineral density values
were significantly lower

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.340
among vegetarians
than among nonvegetarians,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.280
just like is the case
with nearly every study

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.500
on bone mineral density
and excess body weight.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.620
But forget clinical significance;
these differences

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.700
even lost statistical significance
upon adjustment for body size

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.000
factors, suggesting that lower
bone mass among vegetarians

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.080
is in larger parts explained by their
lower BMI and waist circumference.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.100
Thus, it's not so much the composition
of the diets of vegetarians and vegans

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.700
as much as it is the fact that
they become so much slimmer.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.660
Now a small but statistically
significant difference remained

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.800
for total lower spine density,
a difference of 0.03.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.140
This was dismissed as having little
clinical relevance, but is that true?

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.920
If you look at the reproducibility
of bone mineral density

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.200
measurements in daily medical
practice, you can see how

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.960
if you do repeat tests back-to-back,
there's some scatter

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.420
in the measurements, and
so a significant difference

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.940
really has to be more
than the inherent variation.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.300
And indeed, expressed as
the smallest detected difference,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.180
you really need a bone
mineral density disparity

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.120
of at least 0.05 at the spine

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.980
before it can be considered
a significant change,

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:27.300
and so indeed, there does appear
to be little clinical relevance.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.940
However, even if vegetarians
and vegans basically

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.400
have the same bone density
at the same weight,

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.220
everyone who is skinny
is at risk.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.120
Low BMI is a risk factor for
fractures, so all persons in

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.000
a low body weight category
consuming any kind of diet

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.823
should be monitored
for osteoporosis.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.420
In our next story, we look
at the decades-old dogma

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.060
that the lack of animal protein
leads to bone loss.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.660
For most of the last century,
a prevailing theory

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.520
within the field of
nutrition was that

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:00.000
by eating acid-forming
foods such as meat

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.760
we were, in essence, at
risk of peeing our bones

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:03.840
down the toilet.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.160
And no wonder!

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.040
Experiments dating
back to 1920

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.820
showed over and over that if
you add meat to the diet

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.980
you get a big spike in
the amount of calcium

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.300
being lost in the urine.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.000
And so this made total sense.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.100
We had known since 1912

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.260
that meat was acid-
forming within the body,

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.000
and how do you buffer acid?

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.080
What are in antacid pills,
anti-acid pills like Tums?

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:28.800
Calcium.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.360
Meat and eggs have a lot of
sulfur-containing amino acids,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.700
2-5 times more than
grains and beans

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:38.760
that are metabolized
into sulfuric acid,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.980
which the body buffers
with calcium.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.040
That's why the antacids like
Tums are made out of calcium,

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.360
calcium can buffer acid.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.580
And where is calcium
stored in the body?

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:49.320
The skeleton.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.100
So the thinking was that
every time we ate a steak

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.200
our body would pull
calcium from our bones,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.980
bit by bit, and over time
this could lead to osteoporosis.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.480
Based on 26 such studies
every 40 grams of protein

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.580
we add to your daily diet we pee
out an extra 50mg of calcium.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.720
And look we only have about 2 pounds
of calcium in our skeleton,

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.360
so the loss of 50
a day would mean

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.120
losing close to 2% of
bone calcium every year.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.720
And so by the end
of the 20th century,

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.600
there was little doubt
that acid-forming diets

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.640
would dissolve our bones away.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.880
But if you actually
look at all the studies

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.680
done on protein intake
and bone health,

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.020
that's not what you find.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.560
So, where's the
flaw in our logic?

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:40.620
Meat leads to acid,
which leads to calcium loss,

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.600
which leads to bone loss, right?

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.520
Well, it's uncontroversial that protein
results in greater calcium excretion,

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:51.360
but we've just been assuming
it's coming from the bone—

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.060
I mean where else
could the extra calcium

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.700
dumped in our urine be coming
from but from our bones?

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.480
This is the study that appeared
to solve the mystery.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.900
An intrepid group of researchers tried
feeding a bunch of volunteers

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.980
radioactive calcium and then putting
them on a high protein diet.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.280
What happens when you put people
on a high protein diet?

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.620
The amount of calcium in
their urine shoots up,

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.300
and indeed that's
just what happened.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.400
But here's the big question:

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.240
Was that extra calcium in their
urine radioactive or not?

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:25.140
And to everyone's surprise
it was radioactive.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:28.440
Meaning, that the excess
calcium in their urine

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.000
was coming from their diet!

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.180
Remember they were feeding
them radioactive calcium.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.480
So the excess calcium in their urine
wasn't coming from their bones,

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.820
but from what
they were eating.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.460
What seemed to be
happening is that

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:45.600
excess protein consumption
boosted calcium absorption,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.060
from down around
19% up to 26%.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.820
So all of a sudden there was all
this extra calcium in the blood

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.440
so presumably the kidneys are like,

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:53.880
"Whoa what are we going
to do with it all?"

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:55.380
So they dump it
in the urine.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:58.800
90% of the extra calcium in the
urine after eating a steak

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.820
doesn't appear to be coming from
our bones but from our diet.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.300
We're not sure why protein
boosts calcium absorption.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.420
Maybe the protein increases
the solubility of calcium

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.460
by stimulating stomach
acid production?

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:14.520
Whatever the reason,
yes, more calcium lost,

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.280
but more calcium gained
such that in the end

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.100
most of that extra
calcium is accounted for.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.700
So in effect more calcium is
lost in the urine stream,

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.520
but may be compensated
by less loss of calcium

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.140
through the fecal stream.

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:32.580
This was repeated with
even more extreme diets—

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.820
an acid-forming five burgers a day
worth of animal protein diet

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.860
that limited fruits
and vegetables

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.520
versus an alkaline diet emphasizing
fruits and vegetables.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.100
More calcium in the
urine on burgers,

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.920
but significantly greater
calcium absorption

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.960
such that at the end it
was pretty much a wash.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:53.640
Other studies have also since
supported this interpretation.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:54.900
Here's an ingenious one:

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.600
Feed people a high
animal protein diet,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:00.960
but with an alkali salt
to neutralize the acid.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.180
The old thinking would
predict that there

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:06.960
would be no calcium loss since there
is no excess acid to buffer.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.560
But no! Even though the
acid load was neutralized

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.660
there was still the
excess urinary calcium,

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.720
consistent with the
radioactive isotope study,

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:20.460
challenging this long-standing dogma
that animal protein consumption

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:25.469
results in an acidosis that promotes
the excess excretion of calcium.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:30.000
Finally today, we look at
how the galactose in milk

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.560
may explain why
milk consumption

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.720
has been associated with
significantly higher risk

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:37.263
of hip fractures, cancer,
and premature death.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.380
Milk is touted to
build strong bones,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.860
but a compilation of all the
best studies found no association

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.260
between milk consumption
and hip fracture risk,

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.500
so drinking milk as an
adult might not help bones

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.780
but what about
in adolescence?

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.660
Harvard researchers decided
to put it to the test.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.480
Studies have shown that
greater milk consumption

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.593
during childhood and adolescence
contributes to peak bone mass

00:12:00.593 --> 00:12:02.280
and is therefore
expected to help

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.980
avoid osteoporosis and
bone fractures in later life.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:07.200
But that's not
what they found.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.960
Milk consumption during teenage
years was not associated

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:12.480
with a lower risk of hip fracture.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.640
If anything milk consumption
was associated with

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.460
a borderline increase in fracture risk in men.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.820
It appears that the
extra boost in total body

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.180
bone mineral density you get
from getting extra calcium

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:28.920
is lost within a few years even if
you keep the calcium supplementation up.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.120
This suggests a partial explanation
for the long-standing enigma

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.660
that hip fracture rates
are highest in populations

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.640
with the greatest
milk consumption.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:41.820
Maybe an explanation
why they’re not lower,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.160
but why would
they be higher?

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:47.100
This enigma irked a
Swedish research team,

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:50.220
puzzled because studies
again and again had shown

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:53.580
a tendency of a higher risk of fracture
with a higher intake of milk.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:58.440
Well there is a rare birth
defect called galactosemia,

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.900
where babies are born
without the enzymes needed

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:05.280
to detoxify the galactose
found in milk so they end up

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.260
with elevated levels of
galactose in their blood,

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.480
which can causes bone
loss even as kids.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.020
So maybe, the Swedish
researchers figured,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:16.320
even in normal people that
can detoxify the stuff,

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:19.260
it might not be good for the bones
to be drinking it every day.

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:21.660
And galactose doesn’t
just hurt the bones.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:25.500
That’s what scientists use to cause
premature aging in lab animals.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.240
They slip them a
little galactose

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:28.740
and you can shorten
their lifespan,

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:31.560
cause oxidative stress,
inflammation, brain degeneration,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:37.620
just with the equivalent of like 1-2 glasses
of milks worth of galactose a day.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.420
We’re not rats,
though—

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:42.240
but given the high amount
of galactose in milk,

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:46.320
recommendations to increase milk
intake for prevention of fractures

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.180
could be a conceivable
contradiction,

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:51.240
so they decided
to put it to the test,

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.540
looking at milk
intake and mortality

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.060
as well as fracture risk
to test their theory.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.640
A hundred thousand
men and women

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.020
followed for up
to 20 years-

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.460
what did they find?

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.460
Milk drinking women
had higher rates

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:09.360
of death, more heart disease, significantly
more cancer for each glass of milk.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:13.800
Three glasses a day was associated
with nearly twice the risk of death.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.640
And they had significantly more
bone and hip fractures too.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.180
Men in a separate study
also had a higher rate

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.280
of death with higher
milk consumption

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.220
but at least they didn’t have
higher fracture rates.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.720
So a dose dependent higher rate
of both mortality and fracture

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.140
in women and a higher rate of
mortality in men with milk intake,

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:39.000
but the opposite for other dairy
products like soured milk and yogurt,

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.520
which would go along with
the galactose theory,

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.420
since bacteria can ferment
away some of the lactose.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.360
To prove it though, we need
a randomized controlled trial

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.300
to examine the effect of milk intake
on mortality and fractures.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.120
As the accompanying
editorial pointed out,

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:58.140
we better find this out
soon as milk consumption

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.978
is on the rise
around the world.

